The Shop Manager has a surprisingly broad foundation, but it is not yet a finished production repair-shop system. Many screens and components exist; the biggest gap is that they are not all connected into one tested end-to-end workflow.
What already exists:
Customers and vehicles
Work orders and service board
Quotes and invoices
Payments
Inventory, suppliers, purchase orders, and parts tracking
Scheduling, bookings, calendar, and planner
Technician portal, timesheets, payroll, and team roles
Service packages, labor rates, repair plans, and reminders
Customer communications and customer portal
Reports and analytics
Vehicle damage diagrams
Inspection components and three routed inspection prototypes
Tire, fleet, equipment, warranty, and maintenance sections
Automotive, marine, fuel-delivery, and welding modules
The most important missing or incomplete areas are:
Production digital inspections
The three new inspection forms currently run as HTML prototypes and save locally in the browser. They are not yet connected to:
Supabase
Customer and vehicle records
Work orders
Technician accounts
Image storage
Customer approval
Inspection history
Estimate recommendations
This should be the highest priority.
One reliable repair-order workflow
We need to verify and standardize the complete process:
Appointment
→ Customer and vehicle
→ Check-in and damage recording
→ Digital inspection
→ Technician diagnosis
→ Estimate
→ Customer authorization
→ Parts and labor
→ Work in progress
→ Quality control
→ Invoice and payment
→ Vehicle release
→ Follow-up reminder
Individual pieces exist, but the entire chain needs integration testing and cleanup.
Estimate authorization
A professional shop system needs:
Approve or decline individual jobs
Customer signature
Date, time, and IP/audit record
SMS/email approval link
Revised estimate authorization
Declined-work history
Deposit request
Authorization limits for fleet accounts
Technician workflow
The technician-facing experience needs to be confirmed or completed:
Assigned job queue
Clock in/out per labor operation
Pause reasons
Parts requests
Inspection completion
Photos and technician notes
Job completion submission
Comeback identification
Productivity and efficiency calculations
Parts-to-work-order integration
Inventory functionality exists, but it must operate reliably with repair orders:
Reserve parts for a work order
Issue and return parts
Special-order parts
Core charges and core returns
Purchase-order receiving
Partial deliveries
Superseded part numbers
Pricing matrices and markup
Low-stock ordering
Lost, damaged, and adjusted inventory
Serialized parts and warranty tracking
Checkout and vehicle release
A focused front-counter checkout is needed:
Final invoice review
Inspection/quality-control confirmation
Payment or partial payment
Multiple payment methods
Outstanding balance
Customer signature
Vehicle release authorization
Receipt delivery
Keys returned
Final mileage and fuel level
Shop-specific accounting controls
Accounting components exist, but shop operations need:
Cash drawer and daily close
Tax handling by location
Deposits and refunds
Accounts receivable
Fleet statement billing
Vendor bills
Parts, labor, sublet, fees, and shop-supply accounting
Sales and payment reconciliation
QuickBooks/Xero export or integration
Warranty and comeback management
The system should track:
Parts warranty
Labor warranty
Supplier warranty claims
Customer complaints
Comeback work orders
Original technician and repair
No-charge warranty labor and parts
Root cause and management review
Sublet work
Repair shops frequently outsource machining, alignment, towing, glass, upholstery, programming, and specialty work. It needs:
Sublet vendor
Purchase cost
Customer selling price
Expected return date
Vendor purchase order
Attachment to the repair order
Data safety and deployment readiness
Before relying on it commercially, it needs:
A separate production database
Development/staging database
Verified tenant isolation between shops
Row-level security testing
Storage policies for inspection photos and documents
Automated backups
Restore testing
Error monitoring
Audit logs
Permission testing
Automated tests for critical workflows
There is also still inherited general-business code—shopping, affiliate tools, AI demonstrations, enterprise features, generic equipment/safety systems, and developer tooling. Some may be useful later, but they increase complexity. The cleaner approach is to hide or remove anything that does not serve the primary repair-order workflow.
My recommended build order is:
Finish the shared digital-inspection system.
Connect inspections to customers, vehicles, technicians, and work orders.
Complete estimates and customer authorization.
Test the full repair-order lifecycle.
Complete parts issuing, ordering, receiving, and returns.
Finish checkout, payment, and vehicle release.
Add warranty, comeback, and sublet workflows.
Harden permissions, database isolation, backups, and tests.
Remove remaining inherited features that are not needed.
